// expect: runtime_error
// expect-error: E1009

// GAP — owner: roadmap E2 (0185 stage 4 / R6b), 0.0.8.
// Measured 2026-09-11 on the VM: error[E1009] panic: No instance of Def.zero
// for the given type.
//
// NOTE: this file compiles. The gap is at run time, which is why it lives
// here rather than in ../compile/.
//
// `zero()` takes no argument, so nothing at the call site determines `a`, and
// `let x = zero()` never uses the result in a way that would. Two instances
// are in scope. This is *ambiguity*, and it is what E2 exists to report:
// "a predicate reaching whole-program scope over an unresolved variable is
// reported with its origin".
//
// What happens instead is that the program compiles and the dispatch stub
// panics at run time with a message that describes the wrong thing — it says
// no instance matched, when the real problem is that two did and nothing
// chose between them. A reader given this message would go looking for a
// missing instance.
//
// E2 is **blocked on B2**, and B2 moved to 0.0.8, so this moves with it. The
// stage's premise is that once generalize-by-arity has landed the residue is
// ambiguity rather than stranded obligations; until then the residue is still
// the old kind and the report would be wrong.

class Def<a> {
    fn zero() -> a
}

instance Def<Int> {
    fn zero() { 0 }
}

instance Def<String> {
    fn zero() { "" }
}

fn main() with IO {
    let x = zero()
    print("reached")
}
